COMMAND NAME: mppaddmirrors.sh

Adds mirror segments to a Greenplum Database system that was 
initially configured without mirroring.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppaddmirrors.sh [-d <master_data_directory>] [-p <port_offset>] [-s] 
[-a] [-m <mirror_config_file>] [-l <logfile_directory>] [-D] [-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppaddmirrors.sh script configures mirror segment instances for 
an existing Greenplum Database system that was initially configured 
with primary segment instances only. The script will shut down your 
Greenplum Database system while it creates the mirrors, and then restart 
it with mirroring enabled.

CREATING MIRROR DATA DIRECTORIES

By default, the script will prompt you for the data storage location(s) 
where it will create the mirror segment data directories. The script 
will create a unique data directory for each mirror segment instance in 
this location using a predefined naming convention. There must be the same 
number of data directories declared for mirror segment instances as for 
primary segment instances. It is OK to specify the same directory name 
multiple times if you want your mirror data directories created in the 
same location, or you can enter a different data location for each mirror. 
Enter the absolute path. For example:

Enter mirror segment instance directory 1 of 2 > /gp_mirror

Enter mirror segment instance directory 2 of 2 > /gp_mirror

OR

Enter mirror segment instance directory 1 of 2 > /dbfast3/gpmirror

Enter mirror segment instance directory 2 of 2 > /dbfast4/gpmirror

Alternatively, you can run the mppaddmirrors.sh script and supply a 
configuration file containing the mirror host names and data directory 
locations (-m option). This is useful if you want your mirror segments 
on a completely different set of hosts than your primary segments. 
The format of the mirror configuration file is:

<mirror_hostname>:<mirror_data_directory_location>

The <mirror_hostname> must match the host name configured for the machine 
(as returned by the hostname command). Also, you must supply the exact 
same number of segment hosts and segment instances as you have primary 
hosts and primary instances. The host names can either be all the same 
as your primary segment host names, or be all different from the 
primary segment host names.

You must make sure that the user who runs mppaddmirrors.sh (for example, the 
gpadmin user) has permissions to write to these directories. You may want 
to create these directories on the segment hosts and chown them to 
the appropriate user before running mppaddmirrors.sh.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master data directory. If not specified, the value set for 
$MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-p <port_base>

This number is used to calculate the port base used for mirror segments. 
This number is added to whatever number you have chosen for your primary 
segment port base. The default offset is 1000. So for example, if your 
primary segments use a port base of 50000, then mirrors will use a port 
base of 51000 by default. If a host has multiple mirror segment instances, 
the base port number will be incremented by one for each additional 
mirror segment instance started on that host.



-s (spread mirrors)

Spreads the mirror segments amongst the available hosts. The default is 
to group the a set of mirror segments together on an alternate host from 
their primary segment set. Mirror spreading will place each mirror on a 
different host within the Greenplum Database array. Spreading is only 
allowed if there is a sufficient number of hosts in the array 
(number of hosts is greater than or equal to the number of segment instances).

-a (do not prompt)

Run in quiet mode - do not prompt the user to enter information. 
Must supply a configuration file with -m if this option is used.


-m <mirror_config_file>

A configuration file containing one line for each mirror segment you 
want to create. You must have the same number of mirror hosts and segments 
as you do primary hosts and segments. Each line in the file specifies 
the mirror host name and data directory location of a mirror segment 
in the format of: 
<mirror_hostname>:<mirror_data_directory_location>


-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging output to debug level.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Add mirroring to an existing Greenplum Database system using the same 
set of hosts as your primary data:

mppaddmirrors.sh -d /dbfast1/gp-1 -p 10000


Add mirroring to an existing Greenplum Database system using a different 
set of hosts from your primary data:

mppaddmirrors.sh -d /dbfast1/gp-1 -p 10000 -m mirror_config_file

Where the mirror_config_file looks like this:

host1:/dbfast2/mirror
host1:/dbfast4/mirror
host2:/dbfast2/mirror
host2:/dbfast4/mirror
host3:/dbfast2/mirror
host3:/dbfast4/mirror


